type: software
title: "Domination polynomials with many peaks: counterexamples to the Alikhani–Peng conjecture and the minimum-degree scale for a central mode"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. A set of vertices dominates a graph when every vertex is in the set or next to it; counting dominating sets by size gives the coefficients d_k of the domination polynomial. Alikhani and Peng conjectured in 2009 that this sequence is always unimodal. Two explicit connected graphs on 317,440 vertices, one split and one bipartite, satisfy d_160080 > d_160320 < d_160560, proved by an integer-arithmetic certificate. The same two-family construction gives graphs with order sqrt(n)/log n peak regions, graphs whose most frequent sizes are at distance of order n from the average size (and, explicitly, more than 88 from it for the 317,440-vertex split graph), and counterexamples at minimum degree (1/2 + o(1)) log2 n. Conversely, minimum degree log2 n + log2 log2 n + 1 forces unimodality with the maximum at ceil(n/2), which is best possible up to an additive constant for the position of the maximum."
